WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.360
August 2021 saw the first trailer for "No Way&nbsp;
Home" hit the internet, and, suffice to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.080
fans absolutely lost their minds. They were&nbsp;
met with returning villains, head-scratching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.800
revelations, and more ... but it wasn't enough.&nbsp;
It didn't take long for the Marvel fandom to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.600
call for a second teaser, and at long last,&nbsp;
Marvel Studios and Sony Pictures have obliged.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.960
Aside from giving us a better sense&nbsp;
of what to expect from the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.200
our latest look at "No Way Home" is packed&nbsp;
with small details that you might have missed.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.360
The "No Way Home" trailer was full of interesting&nbsp;
parallels that served as callbacks to former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.920
Spidey flicks. Perhaps the biggest reference to an&nbsp;
older film came toward the end of the trailer when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.520
MJ falls off some scaffolding, and it looks like&nbsp;
Peter may or may not be able to save her in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.560
We see their hands almost come together, but we'll&nbsp;
have to wait for the movie to see if MJ is saved.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.720
Fans of "The Amazing Spider-Man 2" were&nbsp;
likely taken back to the tragic moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.520
Gwen Stacy dies in the film after falling from&nbsp;
a clocktower. Peter tries his best to save her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.680
but even though his web latches onto her at the&nbsp;
last second, it doesn't do so in enough time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.480
to prevent her from hitting the ground. The&nbsp;
camera work in "No Way Home" shows MJ falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.840
the exact same way — zooming in on her face&nbsp;
with wide eyes as she plummets in slow motion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.720
Let's just hope MJ's fall has a&nbsp;
much better outcome. RIP Gwen.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.760
It's worth noting that the&nbsp;
first MJ actress, Kirsten Dunst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.800
also had a dramatic falling scene in 2002's&nbsp;
"Spider-Man," though thankfully, she survived it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.840
Fingers crossed that the same&nbsp;
holds true this time around.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.680
As virtually all of the "Spider-Man: No&nbsp;
Way Home" marketing material has revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.720
Tom Holland's Spidey will do battle with villains&nbsp;
from across multiple "Spider-Man" film franchises.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.040
Yes, even the widely-panned "Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man" series is getting in on the action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.760
with Rhys Ifans' Lizard and Jamie Foxx's&nbsp;
Electro returning for another go-around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.120
The former Dr. Curt Connors looks largely the same&nbsp;
as he did in 2012's "The Amazing Spider-Man," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.320
that sentiment doesn't apply to his electrifying&nbsp;
companion, who's sporting an entirely new look.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.440
When "The Amazing Spider-Man 2" hit theaters&nbsp;
in 2014, fans weren't very keen on Electro's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.320
updated appearance. The glowing blue skin and&nbsp;
black bodysuit didn't remind fans of the fabled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.040
Spider-Man foe in the least, echoing the visuals&nbsp;
of the "Ultimate Spider-Man" comics version more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.280
than the classic incarnation that everyone loved.&nbsp;
Thankfully, it seems that "No Way Home" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.160
corrected this creative misstep by giving Max&nbsp;
Dillon a more Steve Ditko-inspired aesthetic.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.800
The second trailer showed off the new-and-improved&nbsp;
Electro in all of his lightning bolt-masked glory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.800
ditching the neon blue altogether.&nbsp;
Of course, this begs the question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.880
is this version of the character even the same&nbsp;
as the one from the "Amazing Spider-Man" sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.960
or — if we look to the Disney+ series&nbsp;
"Loki" for multiverse clues — is it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.520
variant Electro? We'll just have to wait and see.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.040
One of the big focuses of the new&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" trailer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.400
is on how all of these villains are&nbsp;
"supposed" to die fighting Spider-Man.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:52.960
“They all die fighting Spider-Man. Sorry kid.”

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.040
And with Tom Holland's Spidey being the&nbsp;
good guy that he is, it seems — or at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.440
the trailer implies — that he wants to save&nbsp;
them, in some way, even as he battles them.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.400
Perhaps this points to the antagonists of "No Way&nbsp;
Home" not being the old villains. What if there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.680
a bigger foe behind all of this? After all, Alfred&nbsp;
Molina's Doc Ock seems oddly mentor-like to Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.240
in the trailer, which fits with his sympathetic&nbsp;
interpretation in "Spider-Man 2," as well.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.080
Of course, it's important to note that&nbsp;
trailers for MCU films are notoriously filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.960
with misdirection, so who knows if any of what's&nbsp;
shown here is what actually happens in the film.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.720
Another factor to consider here is the&nbsp;
role of Doctor Strange in the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.400
While he seems to genuinely want to help&nbsp;
Peter fix the mess that they've made together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.720
he also seems to be at odds with Peter's apparent&nbsp;
mission to save the villains from their fate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.200
Will a big conflict brew between the two? Is&nbsp;
Doctor Strange more of an antagonist than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.920
supporting character, here? Again, misdirection&nbsp;
is a big tool in the MCU trailer playbook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.200
but it would be an interesting direction&nbsp;
for the film to take, nonetheless.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.720
Of all the shocking and exciting new&nbsp;
reveals in the newest "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.400
No Way Home" trailer, we arguably&nbsp;
get the most from Otto Octavius.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.880
“Wait now seriously what’s your actual name?”

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.400
However, while Doc Ock himself&nbsp;
is wonderfully familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.880
there's something different&nbsp;
about his famous tentacles.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.880
This change appears during two scenes with Tom&nbsp;
Holland's Spider-Man in the trailer. When you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.600
look closely, the tentacles seem to have a coating&nbsp;
of red that was not present in "Spider-Man 2,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.800
a color that could indicate Stark technology. The&nbsp;
reason for this would be unclear, but probably has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.280
something to do with Doc Ock's arrival in the&nbsp;
MCU. For instance, did he steal Stark tech from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.320
Peter's Spider-Man costume, and rework it to fit&nbsp;
his tentacles? If anyone could one-up Tony Stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.560
it would be the brilliant Octavius. Did he&nbsp;
break into a Stark facility and sneak a peek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.040
himself? Either way, an upgraded Doc Ock is&nbsp;
not something Spidey should be happy to see.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.800
In the first trailer for "No Way Home,"&nbsp;
there were subtle hints at the villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.320
we would see in the film. The only sure&nbsp;
thing was the appearance of Doc Ock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.720
who showed up swinging his metal tentacles&nbsp;
like the good old days. Sandman, Electro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.560
Green Goblin, and Lizard were hinted&nbsp;
at, but we never saw them outright.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.440
That all changed in the second trailer. Here, we&nbsp;
have been given head-on views of all four baddies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.480
and we even hear some dialogue&nbsp;
from Electro and Green Goblin.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:01.680
“You’re not going to take this away from me…”

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.040
“Peter, you’re struggling.”

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.320
This surely confirms that Peter will&nbsp;
be taking on a group of supervillains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.640
long rumored to be the Sinister Six.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.120
But if so, who is the sixth villain? For&nbsp;
that matter, will there be a sixth villain?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.560
The comic book supervillain team&nbsp;
surely isn't called the Sinister Five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.240
but if there's a sixth bad guy in the&nbsp;
mix, then Sony and Marvel Studios have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.760
done a fabulous job of keeping&nbsp;
this mystery character just that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.280
The biggest fan speculations online suggest the&nbsp;
sixth member is Vulture, Mysterio, or Venom. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.840
have solid chances of showing up in the film, and&nbsp;
maybe we'll get more than six villains, after all.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.520
One of the most incredible shots from&nbsp;
the "No Way Home" trailer comes toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.280
the end when Peter is flying in the air&nbsp;
toward Electro, Sandman, and Lizard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.200
While we'd never rule our Spidey out, it's&nbsp;
an unfair fight, and it doesn't look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.840
Peter stands a chance. Luckily, it seems&nbsp;
like this is not a three-on-one battle.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.040
Based on the position of the characters&nbsp;
in this scene, where they are facing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.280
and where they appear to be heading, it definitely&nbsp;
looks like this is a three on three battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.600
The only villain that seems to be going for&nbsp;
Peter is Sandman, who is staring him straight on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.120
Lizard's positioning makes it looks like&nbsp;
he's going to breeze right past Peter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.360
and Electro seems to be facing upward, away&nbsp;
from the Web-Slinger. This very well could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.600
the scene when Peter and two other Spider-Men —&nbsp;
that would be Tobey Maguire and Andrew Garfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.920
if the past year of rumors is correct – team&nbsp;
up and attack the supervillains together.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.240
Marvel trailers are known for editing out&nbsp;
characters and objects, and it's highly probable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.480
they were removed from this pivotal scene in&nbsp;
order to save their appearance for the final film.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.320
The MCU's Spider-Man is no stranger to overcoming&nbsp;
insurmountable odds and villains of all shapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.960
and sizes. He has already put Adrian Toomes&nbsp;
— better known as The Vulture — behind bars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:45.200
and thwarted Quentin "Mysterio" Beck's evil&nbsp;
schemes, in addition to bravely squaring off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.280
with Thanos multiple times. However, it's&nbsp;
clear that he's biting off a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.760
than he can chew in "No Way Home" by standing&nbsp;
up to multiple super-powered rogues at once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.560
To make matters worse, the loud-mouthed J. Jonah&nbsp;
Jameson will be there to watch it all go down.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.880
After making The Daily Bugle's editor-in-chief&nbsp;
a highlight in director Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.760
trilogy, J.K. Simmons returned as a new version&nbsp;
of J. Jonah Jameson via "Far From Home."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.400
Instead of barking orders at his newspaper staff,&nbsp;
he's now a desk-pounding talk show host with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.000
clear dislike for Spider-Man. His 2019 comeback&nbsp;
was a huge surprise to moviegoers, leading them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.040
to wonder if it was a mere one-off, or if Simmons&nbsp;
was in for the long haul. The second "No Way Home"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.440
trailer supplied the answer by showing fans a&nbsp;
quick glimpse of the MCU's scowling, mustachioed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.920
Jameson on a city street. Time will tell how&nbsp;
much screen time he'll score this time around.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:36.240
Back when the first trailer for "Spider-Man:&nbsp;
No Way Home" came out, we got a sense of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.120
all the chaos in the film comes to be: Peter asks&nbsp;
Doctor Strange to perform a spell that will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.360
everyone forget who is behind Spider-Man's&nbsp;
mask. While the spell is being conjured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.200
Peter panics that Aunt May, Ned,&nbsp;
and MJ won't remember who he is.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.600
“So MJ will forget everything we’ve been through?”

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.440
“Stop tampering with the spell.”

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:56.838
“Oh my God Ned! He’s my best friend.&nbsp;
My Aunt Mae should really know.”

00:07:56.838 --> 00:07:57.840
“Stop. Talking.”

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:03.840
The "No Way Home" trailer seems to confirm that&nbsp;
the spell doesn't go quite as planned. Or does it?

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:07.920
MJ and Ned are peppered throughout the trailer,&nbsp;
and they all seem to know Peter is Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.960
at various points in time. Granted, no trailer is&nbsp;
ever put together in chronological order, so this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.080
can't be a definite, but it does look like Doctor&nbsp;
Strange could not complete his spell — or that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:23.040
if he did so, the loved ones who Peter did name&nbsp;
were somehow spared from the great memory loss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.640
Either way, given the fact that there's now&nbsp;
a bunch of multiverse madness going on, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.360
appears as if Peter's non-stop chattering during&nbsp;
the conjuring really did blow things wide open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.800
and it probably would have been best to&nbsp;
keep his mouth shut ... assuming, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.240
that this isn't Marvel once again&nbsp;
using the trailers to misdirect us.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.000
"Spider-Man: No Way Home"&nbsp;
hits theaters on Dec. 17.

